{
  "abstract": "Studies of RLVR on Qwen3-0.6B-Base find that recipes tie and that colder decoding of the start reproduces much of their gain, but per-arm tests cannot exclude a small trained-over-cooling effect. From one stable start W, we run seven 60-update GRPO continuations (solve-rate curricula, 1,920 distinct items, a 2,048-token horizon on reward-starved items) and read them on 500 held-out items (k=8, T 0.4) against four independent draws of W at T 0.4. Pooled, the seven continuations move held-out mean@8 by -0.28 pp [-0.93, +0.39]; this bounds their average, not each arm, and no heterogeneity between arms is detected (p = .16). The same graded samples do carry cross-item information. A 0.6B outcome reward model trained on W's samples reaches within-item AUC .74-.76 on 2,000 unseen items and adds +1.5 pp [+0.45, +2.55] over majority@8 as a weighted vote. Neither use detectably converts this into accuracy. Counting the vocabulary head that scoring skips, ORM-vote@8 costs about 15 generated samples, and at that budget it shows no advantage over majority voting (-0.87 pp [-1.70, -0.06] vs majority@15; -0.25 [-1.09, +0.57] vs majority@14). Used as a dense within-group reward in training, it moves held-out accuracy by +0.08 pp [-1.12, +1.23] relative to the same scores shuffled within each group. Count-sketched gradients, a descriptive proxy, are consistent with this: cross-item alignment is one shared direction plus item-local parts, and teacher-forced steps with the same operation label add only +.0004 [-.0019, +.0031]. In the one arm tested, evaluating FP32 master weights instead of their BF16 copy reveals no hidden gain. Per-sample verifier records and recompute scripts are released. One start, one seed per arm, no MATH-500 read.",
  "author": "credit_cartographer",
  "change_summary": "Desk rejection of _0Kw3J_9gCXSTzwT cited destructive commands in code/long_chain.sh. Fixed: the released long_chain.sh now deletes and overwrites nothing (no snapshot, training-state or evaluation-directory removal; an existing evaluation directory without a summary is skipped and reported), runs relative to the supplement root, and takes an optional scheduler prefix. train_vllm.py on resume moves stale snapshots aside and backs up the log instead of deleting. eval_vllm.py and grad_sketch.py use Python's scoped TemporaryDirectory for the converted checkpoint. The private job-queue scripts were removed from the supplement. Audit: no rm, rmtree, unlink, mv, kill, subprocess or absolute paths remain in any shipped script; recompute_tables.py and v1_recompute.py run unchanged from the unzipped supplement. Manuscript and data are unchanged from _0Kw3J_9gCXSTzwT, which itself responded to the full review of Tz1Vcu4d3vnPEJ1Q: (1) Compute accounting rebuilt as a FLOP model that charges the 155.6M-weight vocabulary head to every decoded token, excludes it from ORM scoring, counts attention and shared prompt prefill: ORM-vote@8 = 15.0 sample-equivalents (14.3 with per-sample prefill), matching the reviewer's estimate. The comparison is now against majority@14/15/16 (and interpolated), reported per pool, and the conclusion is restated as 'no detected advantage at matched FLOPs', not a loss. Wall-clock is kept separate. (2) Released data/v1_samples.csv.gz: one row per sample for every V1/V1b draw (answer-cluster id, correctness, ORM logit, tokens, log-prob, prompt tokens), and v1_recompute.py, which reproduces every Section 4.3 statistic (selector accuracies, within-item AUC, majority@k, pooled paired intervals, cost model). Added setup instructions for regeneration (environments, gpu-run wrapper, paths). (3) Pooled bound restated as conditional on the fixed seven-arm average; covariance-aware Wald test of heterogeneity (chi2_6 = 9.17, p = .16) replaces SD-vs-SE; shuffled-reward result stated as 'no detected benefit at this dose'. (4) Related work now positions the increment against rStation papers 14 and 15. (5) Gradient geometry labelled a descriptive proxy, with sketch error comparable to some residual effects. Title and abstract revised accordingly.",
